 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q. A newly admitted sheet enters `A1in`, a FIFO queue for first-use entries. A sheet accessed again while still resident promotes to `Am`, a recency-ordered queue for reused entries; a hit within `Am` moves the entry to its most-recent position. Both queues are bounded by a fraction of the 12 GiB budget. When admitting a completed sheet would exceed the budget, eviction takes from `A1in` first and only draws from `Am` once `A1in` is empty, so a long sequential screening displaces its own one-off sheets before it can displace another editor's comparison set. This is the two-queue form of 2Q — there is no separate ghost queue tracking recently evicted first-use keys. The change is confined to this replacement policy: the external interface, queue concurrency, and disk budget are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|--------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (`A1in`/`Am` split, two queues, no ghost queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects an active comparison session's working set from eviction by a concurrent long sequential screening. LRU has the least bookkeeping and adapts fastest when an editor switches projects, but a long scan can evict sheets still in comparison use. Frequency-based aging retains popular clips better but adds counter-maintenance cost and can keep stale favorites resident past a project switch. 2Q accepts the bookkeeping cost of two queues and a size-split parameter to get scan resistance neither alternative gives directly. |
